From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] uio: add a write-combining physical memtype and let
 uio_pdrv_genirq request it

UIO can only hand userspace one page attribute for a physical mapping.
uio_mmap_physical() applies pgprot_noncached() to every UIO_MEM_PHYS map
unconditionally:

  drivers/uio/uio.c:757-759
        vma->vm_ops = &uio_physical_vm_ops;
        if (idev->info->mem[mi].memtype == UIO_MEM_PHYS)
                vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_noncached(vma->vm_page_prot);

For a register file that is exactly right.  For a large memory-like
region shared with a device it is a throughput floor with no way around
it, because on ARMv7 pgprot_noncached() is L_PTE_MT_UNCACHED
(arch/arm/include/asm/pgtable.h:105-106), which the TEX-remap table
encodes as Strongly-ordered (arch/arm/mm/proc-macros.S:135, entry 0x00):
non-bufferable, in-order, one outstanding transaction, so the CPU stalls
per beat.  Measured on a Cyclone V (DE10-Nano), streaming into an FPGA's
shared DDR3 aperture through such a mapping tops out at ~100 MB/s write
and ~54 MB/s read.

This adds UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC, which is UIO_MEM_PHYS mapped with
pgprot_writecombine() instead -- on ARMv7 L_PTE_MT_BUFFERABLE
(arch/arm/include/asm/pgtable.h:108-109), encoded as Normal non-cacheable
(proc-macros.S:136, PTE_EXT_TEX(1)), so stores merge in the write buffer
into bursts.  It is a weaker ordering, not a weaker coherency: the
mapping stays non-cacheable, so a device writing the same memory without
snooping the CPU caches is as safe as it was before.

The change is four files:

  include/linux/uio_driver.h    UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC = 6, appended after
                                UIO_MEM_DMA_COHERENT (memtype defines,
                                :162-173), with the ordering and
                                speculation caveats next to it.
  drivers/uio/uio.c             one else-if in uio_mmap_physical() at the
                                unconditional pgprot_noncached() above,
                                and the new memtype added to the mmap
                                dispatch switch beside UIO_MEM_PHYS
                                (:864-868).
  drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c read a boolean device property once at
                                probe and use it to pick the memtype in
                                the resource loop, which today hardcodes
                                UIO_MEM_PHYS (:203).
  Documentation/driver-api/     the memtype field's own documentation
    uio-howto.rst               (:276-280), which enumerates the values a
                                driver may set.  (That list is already two
                                values behind -- it names neither
                                UIO_MEM_IOVA nor UIO_MEM_DMA_COHERENT --
                                but leaving it three behind is not the fix,
                                and the "upstreamable as is" claim below is
                                only true with this hunk.)

No behaviour change for anything that does not ask for it
---------------------------------------------------------
memtype is chosen by the driver that fills in struct uio_mem, so every
existing UIO driver keeps UIO_MEM_PHYS and reaches the same
pgprot_noncached() line it always did.  The new branch is unreachable
unless a driver sets the new value.  In uio_pdrv_genirq the selector is
device_property_read_bool(), which returns false for a device with no
fwnode, so platform-data-instantiated devices are unaffected as well --
the loop assigns UIO_MEM_PHYS exactly as before.

pgprot_writecombine() is available on every architecture: where the
architecture defines no write-combining attribute, include/linux/pgtable.h
:1793-1795 aliases it to pgprot_noncached(), so a UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC map
degrades to today's uncached mapping rather than failing to build or
mapping something unsafe.

Ordering, and who owns it
-------------------------
A store into a write-combining region is not globally visible when the
instruction retires; it may sit merged in the write buffer, and stores
may reach memory out of order relative to each other.  Any userspace
publish-then-signal sequence over such a mapping therefore needs a
barrier between the payload writes and whatever announces them -- a
doorbell write, a strobe, a ring index.  This is why the attribute is
opt-in per device rather than a global relaxation of UIO_MEM_PHYS, and
why a device with both buffers and ordered registers should describe
them as two devices: an ordered register file must keep the strongly
ordered mapping, and a device-scoped flag makes that separation
structural instead of a review item.

Why a new memtype rather than a flag
------------------------------------
memtype is already the dispatch key in uio_mmap() and already selects
between three mapping implementations; a sixth value costs one switch
label and one else-if.  A separate flags field in struct uio_mem would
add state that every existing driver would have to initialise correctly
to keep its current behaviour, for the same one bit of information.

The device-tree property
------------------------
"mister,map-writecombine" is a vendor property on "generic-uio", which
is itself not a documented binding -- it is a match string injected at
runtime through uio_pdrv_genirq's of_id module parameter
(uio_pdrv_genirq.c:251-258).  The generic half of this patch (the
memtype, its handling in uio.c, and the uio-howto.rst entry) is written
to be upstreamable as is; the property parse is local, and would need a
documented binding and a vendor prefix review before it could go
anywhere.  Kept in one patch here because splitting them would put a
memtype in the tree that nothing in the tree can select.

Consumers
---------
board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches-beta/0044-dts-uio-fpga-regions.patch
adds the two nodes that use this: the register window, which
deliberately does NOT carry the property and so keeps the strongly
ordered mapping, and the shared DDR3 aperture, which does.  Applying
0044 without this patch is safe and inert -- an unknown DT property is
ignored and the aperture maps uncached, i.e. correct but at the floor
above.  That is the intended failure direction: never a cached mapping
of memory an FPGA writes without snooping the A9 caches, which would be
silent corruption rather than lost throughput.

No new kernel config symbol is required; this rides CONFIG_UIO and
CONFIG_UIO_PDRV_GENIRQ, which board/mister/de10nano/linux-rt.fragment
already sets for the RT kernel.

Provenance
----------
Origin:       None.  Originally authored for this tree; no MiSTer fork
              commit corresponds to it (stock MiSTer builds no UIO at
              all).
Author:       Michael C. Ferguson, 2026-08-14.
Upstream:     Not submitted.  The uio.c / uio_driver.h half is written to
              upstreamable standards and could be; the DT property half
              cannot go as is (vendor property on an undocumented
              binding).  Disposition "carry" (docs/patch-provenance.md
              3.1), same posture as 0043 and 0044.
Anchors on:   Linux 7.2-rc7 as built by this variant -- drivers/uio/uio.c,
              drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c, include/linux/uio_driver.h
              and Documentation/driver-api/uio-howto.rst, all unmodified
              upstream files that no other patch in this series touches.

Applies independently of every other patch in the series, at any
position. The relationship to 0044 is semantic, not apply-order: the
mister,map-writecombine property 0044 declares is deliberately inert on
a kernel without this patch (an unknown DT property is ignored), and
this patch changes nothing for nodes that do not declare it.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
---
 Documentation/driver-api/uio-howto.rst |  9 +++++++++
 drivers/uio/uio.c                      |  3 +++
 drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c          | 12 +++++++++++-
 include/linux/uio_driver.h             | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/Documentation/driver-api/uio-howto.rst
+++ b/Documentation/driver-api/uio-howto.rst
@@ -278,6 +278,15 @@
    mapped. Use ``UIO_MEM_LOGICAL`` for logical memory (e.g. allocated
    with :c:func:`__get_free_pages()` but not kmalloc()). There's also
    ``UIO_MEM_VIRTUAL`` for virtual memory.
+
+   ``UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC`` is ``UIO_MEM_PHYS`` mapped write-combining rather
+   than uncached, for memory-like regions such as buffers, framebuffers
+   and shared apertures. Stores to such a mapping may be merged and
+   reordered, so user space must place a barrier between a write and
+   whatever publishes it, and a device that also has ordered registers
+   must describe those as a separate ``UIO_MEM_PHYS`` mapping. Do not use
+   it for a region where reads have side effects or may not complete:
+   write-combining memory may be read speculatively.
 
 -  ``phys_addr_t addr``: Required if the mapping is used. Fill in the
    address of your memory block. This address is the one that appears in
--- a/drivers/uio/uio.c
+++ b/drivers/uio/uio.c
@@ -757,6 +757,8 @@ static int uio_mmap_physical(struct vm_area_struct *vma)
 	vma->vm_ops = &uio_physical_vm_ops;
 	if (idev->info->mem[mi].memtype == UIO_MEM_PHYS)
 		vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_noncached(vma->vm_page_prot);
+	else if (idev->info->mem[mi].memtype == UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC)
+		vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_writecombine(vma->vm_page_prot);
 
 	/*
 	 * We cannot use the vm_iomap_memory() helper here,
@@ -864,6 +866,7 @@ static int uio_mmap(struct file *filep, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
 	switch (idev->info->mem[mi].memtype) {
 	case UIO_MEM_IOVA:
 	case UIO_MEM_PHYS:
+	case UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC:
 		ret = uio_mmap_physical(vma);
 		break;
 	case UIO_MEM_LOGICAL:
--- a/drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c
+++ b/drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c
@@ -112,6 +112,7 @@ static int uio_pdrv_genirq_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	struct fwnode_handle *node = dev_fwnode(&pdev->dev);
 	struct uio_pdrv_genirq_platdata *priv;
 	struct uio_mem *uiomem;
+	bool map_wc;
 	int ret = -EINVAL;
 	int i;
 
@@ -185,6 +186,15 @@ static int uio_pdrv_genirq_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		}
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Regions of a device flagged this way are mapped write-combining
+	 * rather than uncached.  The property is per device, not per region:
+	 * a device that needs both attributes describes them as two nodes,
+	 * so that a region carrying ordered registers cannot inherit the
+	 * relaxed attribute by accident.
+	 */
+	map_wc = device_property_read_bool(&pdev->dev, "mister,map-writecombine");
+
 	uiomem = &uioinfo->mem[0];
 
 	for (i = 0; i < pdev->num_resources; ++i) {
@@ -200,7 +210,7 @@ static int uio_pdrv_genirq_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 			break;
 		}
 
-		uiomem->memtype = UIO_MEM_PHYS;
+		uiomem->memtype = map_wc ? UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC : UIO_MEM_PHYS;
 		uiomem->addr = r->start & PAGE_MASK;
 		uiomem->offs = r->start & ~PAGE_MASK;
 		uiomem->size = (uiomem->offs + resource_size(r)
--- a/include/linux/uio_driver.h
+++ b/include/linux/uio_driver.h
@@ -171,6 +171,24 @@ extern int __must_check
  * Do not use in new drivers.
  */
 #define UIO_MEM_DMA_COHERENT	5
+/*
+ * UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC is UIO_MEM_PHYS with a write-combining page attribute
+ * instead of an uncached one, for regions where the mapping is memory-like
+ * (buffers, framebuffers, shared apertures) rather than a register file.
+ * Stores may be merged and reordered, so a driver that also has ordered
+ * registers must keep them in a separate UIO_MEM_PHYS mapping, and userspace
+ * must place a barrier between a write to such a region and whatever
+ * publishes it.
+ *
+ * Never use it for a region where reads have side effects or may not
+ * complete: write-combining is Normal memory, from which the CPU is permitted
+ * to read speculatively, so an access no program issued can reach any address
+ * in the mapping.  This is the same discipline ioremap_wc() carries.
+ *
+ * On architectures with no write-combining attribute this falls back to the
+ * uncached mapping (asm-generic pgprot_writecombine).
+ */
+#define UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC	6
 
 /* defines for uio_port->porttype */
 #define UIO_PORT_NONE	0
